{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And you shall give it to Eleazar.</b> And not through Aaron, for he was the cause of the calf. And from the fact that we find that Moses prayed that Eliezer who seeks the law of the cow should be from his loins, and here too Eleazar is mentioned — infer from this that there is an allusion to the help of El, blessed be He, toward purification, as it is written (Ezekiel 36:25): ״וְזָרַקְתִּי עֲלֵיכֶם מַיִם טְהוֹרִים״ etc., for without the help of El, blessed be He, this purification would not avail them alone. And from the fact that it specifically says a cow in its second year, a calf in its first year — infer from this that the cause is twice as great as that which results from it; therefore a person needs all the more to be zealous about removing the cause, as stated. And the reason for sprinkling on the third and on the seventh is because the impurity diminishes and passes away, for they said (Mo'ed Katan 27b): three days for weeping, seven for lamenting. Therefore its sprinkling is at these two times."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "In the phrase פִּי שְׁנַיִם, read as פִּי (from פַּעַם) meaning 'twice,' not the common contraction of פִּי שְׁנַיִם meaning 'twofold.' The word מְסֻבָּב (that which is caused/resulted) is paired with סִבָּה (cause) in the causal sense."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:41:52.614271069Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}